{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>On the side of the altar, northward.</b> And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: the north wind is not encompassed, and a throne of three legs cannot stand, and it would need a fourth leg; therefore the offerings are the leg of the world. And thus Scripture says (Psalms 48:3): ״הר ציון ירכתי צפון״. And from this we learned: 'Which is the place of the slaughter-offerings? The holy of holies — their slaughter is in the north.' There is no doubt that this Tanna knew that the Holy Temple is the place of the offerings, but what he mentioned in the language of a question — 'Which is the place of the slaughter-offerings?' — the intent is: in which direction? And he answers: their slaughter is in the north, for thus our Rabbis of blessed memory proved from this verse mentioned in the matter of the burnt-offering. And what he said, 'and their blood requires sprinkling between the staves' — its meaning is opposite the staves, which is the place where the Shekhinah is there, in the manner of what is written (Song of Songs 1:13): ״בין שדי ילין״ — that is, in the letters בי\"ן שד\"י, by an inversion of its letters, are the two staves of the Ark." "<b>And in the chapters of</b> R. Eliezer: Four winds were created in the world — east, for from there light goes forth to the world; south, for from there dews of blessing and rains of blessing go forth to the world; west, for from there treasuries of snow and treasuries of hail and cold and heat and rains go forth to the world; north, for from there darkness goes forth to the world. And the north wind — He created it but did not finish it. The Holy One, blessed be He, said: whoever says 'I am a god' — let him come and finish this corner that I have left unfinished." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah,</b> 'northward' is the attribute of Gevurah, in order to perfect them toward Him. And this is what we learned: 'the ramp' — the entirety of the structure is called a ramp; 'and the corner to surround' — it is the beginning of the beginning, encompassing all; 'and he comes to it' — in thought — 'to the south-western corner,' which is the attribute of Hesed; and this is what is written (Genesis 12:9): ״ויסע אברם הלוך ונסוע הנגבה״, for the priest would arouse upon Israel the attribute of Hesed, which is His attribute. And 'from the south-western to the south-eastern' is Tiferet of Israel, for from there the mercies go forth; and 'from the east to the north,' in order to perfect for Him the attribute of Gevurah; and 'from north to west,' which is Malkhut — and this is what they said: 'the Shekhinah is in the west' — and from there it branches forth to the world of separate beings, and then the lower world is blessed. And 'from west to south' — which is returning to the place where it began."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {2 "The directional terms for the altar corners (e.g., 'south-western') follow the printed Hebrew as understood literally; the circuit of corners is rendered exactly as the text reads. The divine attributes (Gevurah, Hesed, Tiferet, Malkhut) are transliterated as proper Kabbalistic terms per voice rules; 'Shekhinah' likewise kept as a reverent divine-associated term without translation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:22:36.560378613Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}